findings.body:
**Claimed critical; verifier found:** verifier off

Removing the `BLOCKED` set reopens prototype pollution on `deepMerge`. The claim that `JSON.parse` never yields dangerous keys is false: `JSON.parse('{"__proto__": {"polluted": true}}')` produces an own enumerable `"__proto__"` key, and `Object.entries` surfaces it.

With the guard gone, the recursive branch does `(base as any)["__proto__"] ??= {}` and then assigns into `Object.prototype`. The `constructor`/`prototype` chain works the same way (`{"constructor": {"prototype": {"hacked": true}}}`). Verified in Node against this merge shape: after merge, `({}).polluted` / `({}).hacked` were `true`.

Restore the blocklist (or merge via `Object.hasOwn` + `Object.defineProperty` on `Object.create(null)` targets) before merging any external JSON.

```suggestion
const BLOCKED = new Set(["__proto__", "constructor", "prototype"]);

export function deepMerge<T>(base: T, patch: Record<string, unknown>): T {
  for (const [k, v] of Object.entries(patch)) {
    if (BLOCKED.has(k)) continue;
```
